      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\(s_2\\), we first need to determine the endpoints of segment \\(s_2\\) by applying the given translation to the endpoints of segment \\(s_1\\).\n\n### Step 1: Determine the Midpoint of Segment \\(s_1\\)\nGiven endpoints of segment \\(s_1\\) are \\((4,1)\\) and \\((-8,5)\\), we start by finding the midpoint \\((x_m, y_m)\\) of \\(s_1\\). The formula for the midpoint of a line segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the coordinates of segment \\(s_1\\):\n\n\\[\n(x_m, y_m) = \\left(\\frac{4 + (-8)}{2}, \\frac{1 + 5}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{-4}{2}, \\frac{6}{2}\\right) = (-2, 3)\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Apply the Translation to Find Endpoint of Segment \\(s_2\\)\nSegment \\(s_2\\) is obtained by translating segment \\(s_1\\) by 2 units to the right and 3 units up. This means we add 2 to the \\(x\\)-coordinate and 3 to the \\(y\\)-coordinate of each endpoint of segment \\(s_1\\).\n\nThe new endpoint of segment \\(s_2\\) with the original endpoint \\((4,1)\\) becomes:\n\n\\[\n(4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6,4)\n\\]\n\nThe new endpoint of segment \\(s_2\\) with the original endpoint \\((-8,5)\\) becomes:\n\n\\[\n(-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6,8)\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Find the Midpoint of Segment \\(s_2\\)\nWe now have the endpoints of segment \\(s_2\\) as \\((6,4)\\) and \\((-6,8)\\). Using the midpoint formula again, we find the midpoint \\((x_m, y_m)\\) of segment \\(s_2\\):\n\n\\[\n(x_m, y_m) = \\left(\\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2}\\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nThus, the midpoint of segment \\(s_2\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, 6)}\n\\]",
